import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const fittsCfg = {
  curves: [{ expr: 'a + b*log2(300/W + 1)', label: 'time to hit a 300 px-away target', color: '#06b6d4', width: 2.4 }],
  params: [
    { name: 'a', label: 'reaction floor a', min: 0, max: 0.5, step: 0.05, value: 0.15, unit: 's' },
    { name: 'W', label: 'target width W', min: 10, max: 200, step: 5, value: 40, unit: 'px' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 1200, label: '— (see sliders)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 1.4, label: 'time (s)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 330,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/design/foundational/design-is-deciding">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Design is not decoration — it is <strong>deciding on the user's behalf</strong>: what to see
    first, what happens when you tap, what happens when you're wrong. The craft is making
    hundreds of small decisions so coherently that the interface feels obvious. Three decisions
    dominate: <strong>hierarchy</strong> (what matters most), <strong>affordance</strong> (what
    looks usable), and <strong>feedback</strong> (confirming every action instantly).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Fitts's law (1954) — bigger, closer targets are faster to hit; drag the width and the maths agrees" widget="plotter" config={fittsCfg} />

  <p>
    Fitts's law quantifies a truth every thumb already knows: hitting a target takes time
    proportional to the logarithm of distance-over-size. It is why phone buttons live at the
    bottom of the screen (closest to your thumb), why the close button on a modal is smaller than
    the primary action (you're steering the user's choice), and why tiny tap targets are design
    failures with a formula behind them.
  </p>

  <h2>The working rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One primary action per screen.</strong> If two buttons look equally important, the design has decided nothing.</li>
    <li><strong>Contrast is the lever of hierarchy:</strong> size, weight, colour and space all shout; a design that shouts everything is whispering.</li>
    <li><strong>Whitespace is structure,</strong> not emptiness — it groups related things and separates unrelated ones faster than borders ever will.</li>
    <li><strong>Never let a click land in silence.</strong> Every action gets a visible result (state change, spinner, toast) within ~100 ms or the user retries — usually destructively.</li>
    <li><strong>Error messages are design.</strong> Say what happened, why, and what to do next: "Card declined — try another card or contact your bank", not "Error 402".</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How designers know they're right</h2>
  <p>
    They don't guess: <strong>usability tests</strong> (five users find most severe problems —
    Nielsen's long-standing guideline), analytics for what people <em>do</em>, interviews for
    why. Taste gets you to a candidate; evidence decides. The best designers are the ones most
    willing to be wrong early, on paper, where being wrong is free.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Fitts (1954); Norman, The Design of Everyday Things; Nielsen, Usability Engineering.</p>
</TopicPage>
